Run the desktop-file merger through sh on Windows

Without USE_YAML, lxqt_translate_desktop() makes LXQtTranslateDesktop.sh the
custom command itself, counting on its #!/bin/sh line and execute bit. Windows
honours neither. Ninja runs custom commands through cmd.exe /C, and cmd.exe
hands a .sh to whatever program the extension is associated with -- normally
none -- so the merge never runs. The step still succeeds, and the build carries
on without the .desktop file, which surfaces only later, when something
installs or reads it.

On Windows, look up sh and run the script with it. The lookup sits in the
branch that needs it, so a project that only uses USE_YAML configures as
before. MSYS2 always has a sh, and the find and grep the script calls: all
three come with its base system. Other platforms are unchanged:
LXQT_SH_EXECUTABLE is never set there and expands to nothing.

Upstream: not submitted
Applies-to: all environments (only the WIN32 branch is new)

--- a/cmake/modules/LXQtTranslateDesktop.cmake
+++ b/cmake/modules/LXQtTranslateDesktop.cmake
@@ -76,8 +76,14 @@
                 COMMENT "Generating ${_fileName}.${_fileExt}"
             )
         else ()
+            if (WIN32)
+                # cmd.exe cannot run a shell script as a command: it hands the
+                # file to the program associated with .sh, normally none, and
+                # the step succeeds without having run anything.
+                find_program(LXQT_SH_EXECUTABLE sh REQUIRED)
+            endif ()
             add_custom_command(OUTPUT ${_outFile}
-                COMMAND ${LXQT_CMAKE_MODULES_DIR}/LXQtTranslateDesktop.sh
+                COMMAND ${LXQT_SH_EXECUTABLE} ${LXQT_CMAKE_MODULES_DIR}/LXQtTranslateDesktop.sh
                     ${_outFile}
                     ${_inFile}
                     ${_fileName}
